Title: "Set Theory: Axioms"
Id: "4b9a150c-46bd-49da-83b4-b106f402c3a4"


Text: "The entirety of classical mathematics can be systematically constructed
       from a single primitive notion—the *set*—and a single primitive binary
       relation—*membership*, denoted by $\in$. Numbers, algebraic structures,
       geometric spaces, and continuous functions are not independent
       ontological primitives; rather, each is formally realized as a set
       equipped with relations that are themselves collections of ordered pairs.
       This chapter establishes the axiomatic foundation of this universe, known
       as Zermelo--Fraenkel set theory (ZF), and introduces the fundamental
       operations that govern set formation."
Id: "a5b9d42b-f22f-4fd4-a867-29de537158c3"


Text: "In formal axiomatic set theory, the notion of a set is taken as an
       undefined primitive. We do not define what a set 'is' in terms of simpler
       components; instead, its behavior is entirely characterized by the
       membership relation $\in$, under which every element of a set is itself a
       set. We declare this foundational entity:"
Id: "cbc6feb5-4f5e-41ef-bf59-342c981aa217"


[\set]
Declares: X
satisfies:
. \axiom.of.infinity
. \axiom.of.union#given{X}
. \axiom.of.power.set#given{X}
. \axiom.of.empty.set
. forAll: Y is \set
  then:
  . \axiom.of.extensionality#given{X := X; Y := Y}
  . \axiom.of.pairing#given{X := X; Y := Y}
Requires:
. capability: x_ "in" X :-> \\abstract
Enables:
. from: Y ::= {y__ : ...}
  capability: x_ "in" X :<->: x_ member_of Y
Documented:
. called: "set"
. description: "The primitive ontological entity in Zermelo--Fraenkel set
                theory. A pure set contains only other sets, completely
                identified by its membership relation via extensionality,
                providing the universal canvas for constructing mathematical
                objects."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{17}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{1}
Id: "a0759217-e1f6-412c-982a-0038cd17a3a1"


Text: "Complementary to membership is the assertion that an object fails to
       belong to a given collection. By classical bivalence, non-membership is
       the direct negation of the membership predicate:"
Id: "48b22b9f-2acc-4e80-9148-667489d48ee1"


[x \.not.in./ X]
States:
when:
. x is \\anything
. X is \set
that:
. not: x "in"? X
Documented:
. written: "x? \notin X?"
. description: "The negation of membership: $x \notin X$ asserts that the set
                $x$ is not an element of the set $X$. It is shorthand for the
                statement that $x \in X$ fails to hold."
Id: "756e0f48-1c0e-4e05-b97a-9c4fcd577603"


SectionTitle: "The Empty Set"
Id: "8dc697a3-2ef0-4af9-8852-a994bdd8882d"


Text: "To construct a universe of sets, the deductive machinery requires an
       initial seed upon which subsequent operations can act. The simplest
       imaginable set is the one having no members whatsoever, serving as the
       base layer $V_0$ of the cumulative hierarchy from which all higher sets
       are generated:"
Id: "7a54c26a-ea2e-4e38-ac69-3426f651c4d4"


[\empty.set]
Defines: X is \set
expresses:
. not:
  . exists: Y is \\anything
    suchThat: Y "in" X
Documented:
. called: "empty set"
. written: "\emptyset"
. description: "The unique bottom element $\emptyset = \varnothing$ of the
                subset lattice possessing no members. Serves as the base layer
                $V_0$ of the von Neumann universe and represents the natural
                number $0$."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{17}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{1}
Id: "96908ddf-5fc5-455d-9cfd-eaf0fd6ada38"


Text: "In contrast to the empty set, many mathematical constructions require the
       presence of at least one element: choice functions cannot select from
       empty sets, and algebraic structures require identity elements. We
       distinguish this condition as a refinement:"
Id: "6a19c255-0426-440b-a2e3-c97b1fe06bc4"


[\(nonempty)::set]
Refines: X
satisfies: X != \empty.set
Documented:
. adjective: "nonempty"
Id: "616950f0-7d70-48ff-a5ac-9d5d49272150"


SectionTitle: "The Core Axioms"
Id: "f2e995e5-7f69-49c8-90bc-46d8f77811f1"


Text: "The cumulative hierarchy of sets is governed by the axiomatic postulates
       of Zermelo--Fraenkel theory. To ensure that our formal universe is not
       trivial, the first axiom guarantees the existence of at least one set,
       namely a set with no members:"
Id: "287b3b2f-e440-43b5-98be-b7310daf6aaf"


[\axiom.of.empty.set]
Axiom:
then:
. exists: X is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: Z is \set
    then:
    . not: Z "in"? X
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Empty Set"
. description: "There is a set having no members: for every set $Z$, $Z \notin
                X$. Extensionality makes this set unique, so any construction
                with no members denotes the same empty set."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{17}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{1}
Id: "f2180719-568d-4084-982b-a71c5f195bf6"


Text: "Having secured the existence of a set, we require an identity criterion
       to determine when two sets are identical. Under the extensional viewpoint
       of mathematics, a set possesses no internal ordering, multiplicity, or
       hidden structure: it is completely determined by the elements it
       contains:"
Id: "7f76409d-9233-44bf-984a-ede2358dcee5"


[\axiom.of.extensionality]
Axiom:
given: X, Y is \set
where:
. forAll: Z is \set
  then: (.Z "in"? X.) \.iff./ (.Z "in"? Y.)
then: X = Y
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Extensionality"
. description: "The fundamental identity criterion in set theory: sets with
                identical elements are identical ($X = Y$). Replaces intensional
                definitions with purely extensional equality, asserting that
                sets possess neither internal order nor multiplicity."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{17}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{1}
Id: "6fc3a89f-f8ef-420e-b1b6-6e5ed4905611"


Text: "Extensionality dictates when two given sets coincide, but it cannot
       produce new sets from existing ones. To populate the universe beyond the
       empty set, we require generative principles. The simplest mechanism
       collects any two given sets into an unordered pair, which in particular
       yields singleton sets when the two arguments coincide:"
Id: "7b45fe82-a42a-4591-9453-350baec1df28"


[\axiom.of.pairing]
Axiom:
given: X, Y is \set
then:
. exists: U is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: Z is \set
    then: (.Z "in"? U.) \.iff./ ((.Z = X.) \.or./ (.Z = Y.))
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Pairing"
. description: "The elementary aggregation mechanism forming the unordered pair
                $\{X, Y\}$. When $X = Y$, it generates the singleton $\{X\}$,
                enabling the construction of Kuratowski ordered pairs
                $(X, Y) = \{\{X\}, \{X, Y\}\}$."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{17}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{1}
Id: "3219aca1-fba1-42a6-9a1f-209aa430c677"


Text: "Pairing builds sets of at most two elements. To construct larger
       collections, or to dissolve one layer of containment in a nested
       collection of sets, we require an operation of arbitrary aggregation. The
       union axiom collapses a family of sets into a single set containing all
       their constituent elements:"
Id: "c0f76644-e25f-4e0c-8cf0-6daf86ad59df"


[\axiom.of.union]
Axiom:
given: X is \set
then:
. exists: U is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: Z is \set
    then:
    . have: Z "in" U
      iff:
      . exists: W is \set
        suchThat:
        . W "in"? X
        . Z "in"? W
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Union"
. description: "The monadic flattening operation $\bigcup X$ collapsing one
                level of hierarchical nesting: collects every element belonging
                to at least one member of $X$."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{17}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{1}
Id: "60bcea09-6bf1-4917-b90f-64ceeb941558"


Text: "While pairing and union assemble elements side by side, they cannot
       generate collections of all subcollections. To form the continuum and
       unlock higher cardinalities, we postulate that the collection of all
       subsets of a given set forms a legitimate set in its own right:"
Id: "139b6eca-d1e5-4fae-97a4-1b05d96726f1"


[\axiom.of.power.set]
Axiom:
given: A is \set
then:
. exists: P is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: x is \set
    then: (.x "in"? P.) \.iff./ (.x \:subset?:/ A.)
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Power Set"
. description: "The exponential size-escalation axiom forming
                $\mathcal{P}(A) = 2^A$. By Cantor's diagonal theorem,
                $|\mathcal{P}(A)| > |A|$, guaranteeing an unbounded hierarchy of
                transfinite cardinalities."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{17}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{1}
Id: "8557cdb3-e02f-4b75-aed1-3dfd1cdee895"


Text: "Although the power set operation escalates cardinalities, beginning from
       the empty set produces only hereditarily finite sets. To bridge the chasm
       to the transfinite, we must explicitly postulate the existence of an
       infinite set containing the empty set and closed under successor
       formation, providing the substrate for the natural numbers:"
Id: "9196ed0a-391f-4fb5-af13-ac8c65db0ae9"


[\axiom.of.infinity]
Axiom:
then:
. exists: I is \set
  suchThat:
  . allOf:
    . \empty.set "in"? I
    . forAll: Y is \set
      then: (.Y "in"? I.) \.implies./ (.\set.successor:of{Y} "in"? I.)
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Infinity"
. description: "The transfinite bridge postulating a limit stage closed under
                von Neumann succession $S(Y) = Y \cup \{Y\}$. Guarantees the
                existence of an infinite Dedekind set, providing the foundation
                for $\mathbb{N}$ and arithmetic analysis."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{67}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{44}
Id: "a0f45971-fcc1-419b-bcd5-f4c4717bb37f"
